/**
 * # Active site key
 *
 * Build-time singleton holding the `siteKey` of the site currently
 * being built. It exists so that **card converters** — pure functions
 * that shape collection data into card props, invoked deep inside React
 * card components where threading a `siteKey` prop through every call
 * site would be impractical — can resolve featured state *for this
 * site* rather than "featured on any site."
 *
 * ## Why this is safe as module state
 *
 * Astro SSG builds one site per `astro build` process. `activeSiteKey`
 * is set once (in each site's `SiteShell` frontmatter, which every page
 * renders through) and stays constant for the whole build. There's no
 * concurrency within a build, so a module-level variable is
 * deterministic.
 *
 * ## Graceful fallback
 *
 * When unset (e.g. an entry point that never renders `SiteShell`, like
 * a standalone OG route that hasn't opted in), `getActiveSiteKey()`
 * returns `undefined` and callers fall back to the previous
 * "featured-anywhere" semantics. Nothing breaks; it just isn't
 * site-narrowed in that context.
 *
 * ## Why not thread a prop / use React context
 *
 * Card converters run during React render of individual card islands;
 * a context provider in `SiteShell` doesn't cross island boundaries,
 * and threading a `siteKey` prop through every card component and every
 * page call site is a large, error-prone mechanical change. A build
 * constant set once is the smaller, more reliable hoisted fix.
 */
let activeSiteKey;
/**
 * Set the site key for the current build. Call once per page render
 * path — `SiteShell` is the canonical place, since every page renders
 * through it and its frontmatter runs before the slotted content (the
 * cards) renders.
 */
export function setActiveSiteKey(key) {
    activeSiteKey = key;
}
/**
 * Read the current build's site key. Returns `undefined` when unset,
 * which callers should treat as "no site narrowing" (featured-anywhere).
 */
export function getActiveSiteKey() {
    return activeSiteKey;
}
